    (v_topic_id, 'A 11-week-old infant, born breech, presents for a routine well-child check. Examination reveals asymmetric thigh skin folds and a positive Ortolani maneuver on the left. What is the most appropriate initial imaging modality to confirm the diagnosis?', '["AP radiograph of the pelvis", "Ultrasound of the hips", "MRI of the hips", "CT scan of the pelvis", "Bone scan"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) is most accurately assessed with ultrasound in infants under 4-6 months of age because the femoral head and acetabulum are predominantly cartilaginous and not fully visible on radiographs. A positive Ortolani test (reduction of a dislocated hip) strongly suggests DDH.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #41227: A 4-year-old child presents with a limp that started yesterday morning. Two weeks ago, he had a mild URI. He is well-appearing. He refuses to bear weight fully but has only mild pain with passive hip ROM. Labs show a normal WBC count and an ESR of 15 mm/hr. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Juvenile idiopathic arthritis", "Slipped capital femoral epiphysis", "Legg-Calv\u00e9-Perthes disease", "Septic arthritis", "Transient synovitis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Detailed orthopedic assessment of the pediatric patient is required to identify these specific conditions correctly and avoid serious long-term complications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 8-day-old infant is brought to the clinic for unilateral left clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The parents ask about the treatment plan. According to the Ponseti method, the deformities are corrected with serial casting in a specific sequence. Which component of the deformity is corrected LAST?', '["Adductus", "Equinus", "Cavus", "Supination", "Varus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In the Ponseti method for clubfoot, the CAVE deformities are corrected sequentially: Cavus (elevating the first ray), Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. The equinus correction often requires a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 1-day-old infant is brought to the clinic for bilateral clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The parents ask about the treatment plan. According to the Ponseti method, the deformities are corrected with serial casting in a specific sequence. Which component of the deformity is corrected LAST?', '["Varus", "Supination", "Adductus", "Cavus", "Equinus"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In the Ponseti method for clubfoot, the CAVE deformities are corrected sequentially: Cavus (elevating the first ray), Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. The equinus correction often requires a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #99989: An 14-year-old overweight adolescent undergoes in situ pinning for a stable Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE). At follow-up, the patient complains of severe stiffness and loss of joint space is noted on X-ray. What is this devastating complication?', '["Chondrolysis", "Infection", "Nonunion", "Heterotopic ossification", "Avascular necrosis of the femoral head"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Detailed orthopedic assessment of the pediatric patient is required to identify these specific conditions correctly and avoid serious long-term complications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 9-year-old soccer player presents with posterior heel pain. The pain worsens after running and improves with rest. Examination reveals tenderness upon medial-lateral compression of the calcaneal tuberosity (positive ''squeeze test''). There is tightness of the Achilles tendon. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Achilles tendon rupture", "Calcaneal stress fracture", "Plantar fasciitis", "Tarsal coalition", "Calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Sever disease is a traction apophysitis of the calcaneus where the Achilles tendon inserts. It is a common cause of heel pain in athletic children aged 8-12. The classic exam finding is pain with medial/lateral compression of the heel. Treatment includes heel cups, calf stretching, and NSAIDs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 4-year-old falls from monkey bars and sustains a significantly displaced extension-type supracondylar humerus fracture. Upon examination, the child is unable to make an ''OK'' sign (unable to flex the IP joint of the thumb and DIP joint of the index finger). Which nerve is most likely injured?', '["Axillary nerve", "Ulnar nerve", "Musculocutaneous nerve", "Radial nerve", "Anterior interosseous nerve (AIN)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The anterior interosseous nerve (AIN), a branch of the median nerve, is the most commonly injured nerve in extension-type supracondylar humerus fractures. Injury manifests as an inability to flex the IP joint of the thumb (flexor pollicis longus) and the DIP of the index finger (flexor digitorum profundus), resulting in an inability to make the ''''A-OK'''' sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 9-year-old boy presents with a painless limp that has worsened over several weeks. He occasionally complains of vague right knee and thigh pain. He is afebrile and has limited internal rotation and abduction of the right hip. Radiographs show increased density and a subchondral crescent sign in the femoral head. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Transient synovitis", "Legg-Calv\u00e9-Perthes disease", "Septic arthritis", "Slipped capital femoral epiphysis", "Developmental dysplasia of the hip"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease is idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head, classically seen in boys aged 4-8. It often presents insidiously with a painless limp or referred pain to the thigh/knee. Radiographs progress from sclerosis to fragmentation and reossification. The crescent sign represents a subchondral fracture.'),
