    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-day-old infant is brought to the clinic for unilateral left clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The parents ask about the treatment plan. According to the Ponseti method, the deformities are corrected with serial casting in a specific sequence. Which component of the deformity is corrected LAST?', '["Equinus", "Adductus", "Supination", "Varus", "Cavus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In the Ponseti method for clubfoot, the CAVE deformities are corrected sequentially: Cavus (elevating the first ray), Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. The equinus correction often requires a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 14-year-old female diver complains of insidious onset, mechanical low back pain that is exacerbated by extension activities (e.g., back walkovers). Neurologic exam is normal. Oblique radiographs of the lumbar spine demonstrate a ''collar'' on the ''Scotty dog''. What is the specific anatomic defect in this condition?', '["Herniation of the nucleus pulposus", "Defect in the pars interarticularis", "Fracture of the transverse process", "Congenital failure of segmentation of the lumbar vertebrae", "Defect in the vertebral body endplate (Schmorl node)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Spondylolysis is a stress fracture or defect of the pars interarticularis. It is highly prevalent in adolescent athletes participating in sports involving repetitive lumbar hyperextension. On oblique radiographs, a radiolucent gap in the pars resembles a collar on the neck of the ''''Scotty dog''''.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 13-year-old female diver complains of insidious onset, mechanical low back pain that is exacerbated by extension activities (e.g., back walkovers). Neurologic exam is normal. Oblique radiographs of the lumbar spine demonstrate a ''collar'' on the ''Scotty dog''. What is the specific anatomic defect in this condition?', '["Defect in the vertebral body endplate (Schmorl node)", "Defect in the pars interarticularis", "Fracture of the transverse process", "Herniation of the nucleus pulposus", "Congenital failure of segmentation of the lumbar vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Spondylolysis is a stress fracture or defect of the pars interarticularis. It is highly prevalent in adolescent athletes participating in sports involving repetitive lumbar hyperextension. On oblique radiographs, a radiolucent gap in the pars resembles a collar on the neck of the ''''Scotty dog''''.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 2-day-old infant is brought to the clinic for bilateral clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The parents ask about the treatment plan. According to the Ponseti method, the deformities are corrected with serial casting in a specific sequence. Which component of the deformity is corrected LAST?', '["Cavus", "Supination", "Equinus", "Adductus", "Varus"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In the Ponseti method for clubfoot, the CAVE deformities are corrected sequentially: Cavus (elevating the first ray), Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. The equinus correction often requires a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old child refuses to bear weight on the right leg. The hip is held in flexion, abduction, and external rotation. Temperature is 38.6°C. Laboratory evaluation shows a WBC count of 15,000/mm³ and an ESR of 50 mm/hr. According to the Kocher criteria, what is the probability that this child has septic arthritis rather than transient synovitis?', '["About 50%", "Exactly 0%", "About 25%", "> 90% (Very high probability)", "< 10% (Very low probability)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Kocher criteria differentiate septic arthritis from transient synovitis. The 4 criteria are: non-weight-bearing, fever > 38.5°C, ESR > 40 mm/hr, and WBC > 12,000/mm³. Having all 4 predictors yields a 99% probability of septic arthritis; 3 predictors yield a 93% probability. This child has 4 predictors.');
